ose  advisor and friend to ragnar  as intelligent as he was eccentric floki  served as a counterpoint to ethelstan  for the first few seasons of vikings  cautioning ragnar against his friendship  with the monk as floki feared  ethelstone's influence would pull ragnar  out of favor with the viking gods  the averse relationship between ragnar's  two closest friends served as a constant  point of tension on the show  but floki's strong convictions and  quirky nature also made him a brilliant  character in his own right  one of floki's best moments came at the  end of the show's second season when the  audience had been led to believe that  the boat builder might have betrayed  ragnar  only to discover that vloki's loyalty  never faltered and that his seeming  wavering was all a ruse meant to give  ragnar the upper hand  that only made it much more  disappointing when floki's character  took a sharp nosedive beginning with the  killing of ethelstone in the show's  third season  not only was the act prompted by a  vision that floki attributed to the gods  meaning it lacked any sort of proper  buildup but it also poisoned his  relationship with ragnar going forward  by the time viking's fifth season rolled  around floki was often his own leading a  settlement in iceland  populated by unmemorable characters and  a relatively dull c  plot that only served to distract from  the show's central narrative  ragnar wasn't always the most likable  protagonist but travis fimmel sure knew  how to captivate an audience and when he  was leading the cast of vikings it felt  impossible to look away  based on a legendary viking hero who may  or may not have actually existed  ragnar began the series as a farmer in  the village of katagat  but as the series went on ragnar's quick  eager mind refused to allow  him to remain settled and he soon began  to seek more and more power  even when ragnar was acting unkind and  bull-headed he remained a compelling  character  additionally his relatio