 not  uncommon for even its most beloved  characters to suffer immensely or die  horribly but when the vast majority of  its female characters seem to exist  mostly to be abused objectified  villainized and killed without much  opportunity to define themselves on  their own terms it all becomes a little  hard to ignore  ragnar and his fellow warriors have  faced plenty of opponents over the  course of the show but no rivalry has  been quite as intriguing as that which  unfolded between ragdar and king eckbert  of wessex ekbert was introduced as a  potential antagonist to the vikings in  season 2 and his similarities with  ragnar were established pretty much  immediately  like ragnar he quickly came to treasure  his conversations with ethelston and  through their talks expert developed a  deep fascination with ragnar himself  despite leading opposing kingdoms the  two found one another to be kindred  spirits  seeing in each other similar levels of  intelligence curiosity ambition and  cunning  over several seasons ekberg and ragnar  would briefly join forces only to  inevitably wind up on opposite sides of  the battle every time  yet despite their ongoing conflict the  two kings managed to cultivate an  unlikely yet mutually respectful  friendship  ragnar and ekbert's discussions and  their strange affection for one another  provided some of the richest and most  thought-provoking scenes in the show's  run  although viking's treatment to most of  its women has never been great there's  one character in particular who deserves  a special shout out for being  consistently the worst and that's a late  queen alslug  from her untimely seduction of ragnar in  season 1 to her paternal negligence that  led to the death of not one but two  different characters named siggy  al slug's choices and actions have wound  up driving some of the show's most  infuriating turns it is the gods who  have woven our destinies  [Music]  not ourselves and while al slug is far  from the only c